# Day 02 – Ansible Ad-hoc Commands and Modules

## 1. Ansible Ad-hoc Commands

**Definition:**

Ad-hoc commands are one-time commands used to execute tasks on managed nodes without creating an Ansible playbook.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible <host-pattern> -m <module> -a "<arguments>"
```

**Examples:**

```bash
ansible all -m ping

ansible all -m command -a "hostname"

ansible all -m command -a "uptime"

ansible all -m command -a "free -h"

ansible all -m command -a "df -h"
```

## 2. Command Module

**Definition:**

The command module executes commands on remote managed nodes without using a shell.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "<command>"
```

**Examples:**

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "hostname"

ansible all -m command -a "date"

ansible all -m command -a "uptime"

ansible all -m command -a "free -h"

ansible all -m command -a "df -h"
```

## 3. Shell Module

**Definition:**

The shell module executes commands through the remote shell. It supports shell operators such as pipes, redirects and command chaining.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m shell -a "<command>"
```

**Examples:**

```bash
ansible all -m shell -a "date && hostname"

ansible all -m shell -a "df -h | grep /dev"

ansible all -m shell -a "free -h && uptime"
```

### Difference Between Command and Shell

| Command | Shell |
|---|---|
| Does not use a shell | Uses a shell |
| No pipe support | Supports pipes |
| No redirection support | Supports redirection |
| Suitable for simple commands | Suitable for shell operations |

## 4. Package Module

**Definition:**

The package module manages software packages on Linux managed nodes. It provides a generic interface across supported package managers.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=<package> state=<state>" --become
```

**Examples:**

Install Git:

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=git state=present" --become
```

Install Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=nginx state=present" --become
```

Remove Git:

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=git state=absent" --become
```

Verify Git:

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "git --version"
```

## 5. Service Module

**Definition:**

The service module manages services on remote Linux systems, including starting, stopping, restarting and enabling services.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=<service> state=<state>" --become
```

**Examples:**

Start Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx state=started" --become
```

Stop Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx state=stopped" --become
```

Restart Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx state=restarted" --become
```

Enable Nginx at startup:

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx enabled=yes" --become
```

Start and enable Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx state=started enabled=yes" --become
```

Verify Nginx:

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "systemctl is-active nginx" --become
```

## 6. File Module

**Definition:**

The file module manages files, directories, symbolic links and file permissions on managed nodes.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=<path> state=<state>" --become
```

**Examples:**

Create a directory:

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/devops-lab state=directory mode=0755" --become
```

Create an empty file:

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/devops-lab/test.txt state=touch" --become
```

Change permissions:

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/devops-lab/test.txt mode=0644" --become
```

Delete a file:

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/devops-lab/test.txt state=absent" --become
```

Delete a directory:

```bash
ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/devops-lab state=absent" --become
```

## 7. User Module

**Definition:**

The user module manages user accounts on remote managed nodes.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m user -a "name=<username> state=<state>" --become
```

**Examples:**

Create a user:

```bash
ansible all -m user -a "name=devops state=present" --become
```

Delete a user:

```bash
ansible all -m user -a "name=devops state=absent" --become
```

Verify user:

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "id devops"
```

## 8. Copy Module

**Definition:**

The copy module copies files from the Ansible control node to remote managed nodes.

**Syntax:**

```bash
ansible all -m copy -a "src=<source> dest=<destination>" --become
```

**Examples:**

Create a file on the control node:

```bash
echo "Welcome to Ansible Automation" > test.txt
```

Copy the file to managed nodes:

```bash
ansible all -m copy -a "src=test.txt dest=/opt/devops-lab/test.txt mode=0644" --become
```

Verify copied file:

```bash
ansible all -m command -a "cat /opt/devops-lab/test.txt"
```

## 9. Setup Module

**Definition:**

The setup module collects system information (facts) from managed nodes, including operating system, hostname, network interfaces, memory and processor details.

**Examples:**

Collect all facts:

```bash
ansible all -m setup
```

Collect OS information:

```bash
ansible all -m setup -a "filter=ansible_distribution*"
```

Collect hostname:

```bash
ansible all -m setup -a "filter=ansible_hostname"
```

Collect IP address:

```bash
ansible all -m setup -a "filter=ansible_default_ipv4"
```

## 10. Privilege Escalation (--become)

**Definition:**

The `--become` option allows Ansible to execute tasks with elevated privileges, generally using sudo.

**Examples:**

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=git state=present" --become

ansible all -m service -a "name=nginx state=started" --become

ansible all -m file -a "path=/opt/test state=directory" --become
```

## 11. Idempotency

**Definition:**

Idempotency means that executing the same Ansible task multiple times maintains the desired system state without making unnecessary changes.

**Example:**

```bash
ansible all -m package -a "name=git state=present" --become
```

First execution: Installs Git if it is not installed.

Second execution: No changes if Git is already installed.

## 12. Important Ansible Commands

```bash
# Check Ansible version
ansible --version

# List inventory
ansible-inventory --list

# Test connectivity
ansible all -m ping

# Check hostname
ansible all -m command -a "hostname"

# Check uptime
ansible all -m command -a "uptime"

# Check memory
ansible all -m command -a "free -h"

# Check disk
ansible all -m command -a "df -h"

# Check operating system
ansible all -m command -a "cat /etc/os-release"

# Collect system facts
ansible all -m setup

# Execute command on a specific group
ansible aws_servers -m command -a "uptime"
```

